WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:04.380
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.380 --> 00:00:10.670
Today we are going to simulate thousands of
years of vegetation in a virtual world.

00:00:10.670 --> 00:00:14.170
And I am telling you, this paper is unbelievable.

00:00:14.170 --> 00:00:20.500
Now, normally, if we are building a virtual
world, we don’t really think about simulating

00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:23.160
a physics and biology-based ecosystem.

00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:29.410
Let’s be honest, it’s more like “Yeah,
just throw in some trees and we are done here”.

00:00:29.410 --> 00:00:35.460
But, in reality, the kinds of vegetation we
have in a virtual world should be at the very

00:00:35.460 --> 00:00:40.079
least a function of precipitation and temperature.

00:00:40.079 --> 00:00:44.710
And here at this point, we know that this
paper means business.

00:00:44.710 --> 00:00:49.819
You see, if there is no rain and it’s super
cold, we get a tundra.

00:00:49.819 --> 00:00:53.890
With no rain and high temperature we get a
desert.

00:00:53.890 --> 00:01:00.469
If we keep the temperature high, and add a
ton of precipitation, we get a tropical rainforest,

00:01:00.469 --> 00:01:06.229
and this technique promises to be able to
simulate these, and everything inbetween.

00:01:06.229 --> 00:01:08.760
See these beautiful little worlds here?

00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:10.579
These are not illustrations.

00:01:10.579 --> 00:01:11.880
No-no!

00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:14.880
These are already the result of the simulation
program.

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:15.880
Nice!

00:01:15.880 --> 00:01:22.860
Now, let’s run a tiny simulation with 400
years and a few hundred plants.

00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:23.890
Step number one.

00:01:23.890 --> 00:01:29.500
The first few decades are dominated by these
shrubs, blocking away the sunlight from everyone

00:01:29.500 --> 00:01:37.259
else, but, over time, step two, watch these
resilient pine trees slowly overtake them

00:01:37.259 --> 00:01:39.470
and deny them their precious sunlight.

00:01:39.470 --> 00:01:41.920
And, what happens as a result?

00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:42.920
Look!

00:01:42.920 --> 00:01:47.090
Their downfall brings forth a complete ecosystem
change.

00:01:47.090 --> 00:01:51.329
And then, step three, spruce trees start to
appear.

00:01:51.329 --> 00:01:53.540
This changes the game.

00:01:53.540 --> 00:01:54.540
Why?

00:01:54.540 --> 00:02:01.689
Well, these are more shade-tolerant, and let’s
see…yes, they take over the ecosystem from

00:02:01.689 --> 00:02:02.719
the pine trees.

00:02:02.719 --> 00:02:06.659
A beautifully done story, in such a little
simulation.

00:02:06.659 --> 00:02:09.150
I absolutely love it.

00:02:09.150 --> 00:02:14.720
Of course, any self-respecting virtual world
will also contain other objects, not just

00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:19.020
the vegetation, and, the simulator says, no
problem.

00:02:19.020 --> 00:02:24.050
Just chuck ‘em in there, and we’ll just
react accordingly and grow around it.

00:02:24.050 --> 00:02:28.160
Now, let’s see a mid-sized simulation.

00:02:28.160 --> 00:02:29.420
Look at that.

00:02:29.420 --> 00:02:35.640
Imagine the previous story with the pine trees,
but, with not a few hundred, but, a hundred

00:02:35.640 --> 00:02:37.540
thousand plants.

00:02:37.540 --> 00:02:39.730
This can simulate that too.

00:02:39.730 --> 00:02:41.590
And now comes the final boss.

00:02:41.590 --> 00:02:43.250
A large simulation.

00:02:43.250 --> 00:02:47.850
Half a million plants, more than a thousand
years.

00:02:47.850 --> 00:02:49.190
Yes, really.

00:02:49.190 --> 00:02:55.480
Let’s see the story here in images first,
and then, you will see the full simulation.

00:02:55.480 --> 00:03:01.650
Yes, over the first hundred years, fast-growing
shrubs dominate and start growing everywhere,

00:03:01.650 --> 00:03:07.770
and after a few hundred more years, the slower-growing
trees catch up, and start overshadowing the

00:03:07.770 --> 00:03:10.440
shrubs at lower elevation levels.

00:03:10.440 --> 00:03:20.080
Then, more kinds of trees appear at lower
elevations, and slowly over 1400 years, a

00:03:20.080 --> 00:03:22.080
beautiful, mixed-age forest emerges.

00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:27.630
I shiver just thinking about the fact that
through the power of computer graphics research

00:03:27.630 --> 00:03:31.160
works, we can simulate all this on a computer.

00:03:31.160 --> 00:03:32.280
What a time to be alive!

00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:37.890
And, while we look at the full simulation,
please note that there is more happening here,

00:03:37.890 --> 00:03:41.810
make sure to have a look at the paper in the
video description if you wish to know more

00:03:41.810 --> 00:03:42.810
details.

00:03:42.810 --> 00:03:44.560
Now, about the paper.

00:03:44.560 --> 00:03:47.650
And here comes an additional interesting part.

00:03:47.650 --> 00:03:51.860
As of the making of this video, this paper
has been referred to 20 times.

00:03:51.860 --> 00:03:59.160
Yes, but this paper is from 2019, so it had
years to soak up some citations, which didn’t

00:03:59.160 --> 00:04:00.400
really happen.

00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:07.510
Now note that one, citations are not everything,
and two, 20 citations for a paper in the field

00:04:07.510 --> 00:04:10.360
of computer graphics is not bad at all.

00:04:10.360 --> 00:04:16.510
But every time I see an amazing paper, I really
wish that more people would hear about it,

00:04:16.510 --> 00:04:20.700
and always I find that almost nobody knows
about them.

00:04:20.700 --> 00:04:25.050
And once again, this is why I started to make
Two Minute Papers.

00:04:25.050 --> 00:04:29.550
Thank you so much for coming on this amazing
journey with me.

00:04:29.550 --> 00:04:54.070
Thanks for watching and 
for your generous support, and I'll see you

00:04:54.070 --> 00:05:18.390
next time!

